# Haryana Geography: Landforms, Rivers and Climate

> Haryana is landlocked, mostly flat and surrounded on three sides around
> Delhi, yet its geography questions are full of traps: seasonal rivers
> that vanish in sand, a "semi-desert" in the northwest and the northern
> end of the Aravallis in the south. HSSC CET and police papers ask for
> the highest peak, the Ghaggar and its tributaries, the barrages and the
> climate pattern. Learn the map in four pieces.

## 1. Location, Area and Neighbours

| Item | Fact |
| --- | --- |
| Position | Landlocked state in northern India, roughly between 27°39' and 30°35' N and 74°28' and 77°36' E |
| Area | About 44,212 sq km, nearly 1.4 per cent of India (21st by area) |
| Population (Census 2011) | 2,53,51,462 |
| Northern neighbours | Punjab, Himachal Pradesh and the Union Territory of Chandigarh |
| Eastern neighbour | Uttar Pradesh, separated by the **Yamuna** |
| Western and southern neighbour | Rajasthan |
| Delhi | Enclosed by Haryana on the north, west and south |

[definition]
**Landlocked** means the state has no sea coast. Haryana encloses the
National Capital Territory of Delhi on its north, west and south, which is
why the NCR districts (Gurugram, Faridabad, Sonipat and others) grow so
fast.
[/definition]

Altitude ranges roughly from 200 m on the plains to over 1,000 m in the
northern hills. The **highest point** is **Karoh Peak** (about 1,467 m) in
the Shivalik hills of Morni, Panchkula district.

## 2. Physiographic Regions

| Region | Where | Key feature |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Shivalik hills | North and northeast (Panchkula, Yamunanagar, Ambala) | Lower Himalayan foothills; Morni hills; forests and seasonal torrents |
| Yamuna-Ghaggar plain | Most of the state | Fertile alluvial plain, part of the Indo-Gangetic plain; the agricultural heartland |
| Semi-desert / Bagar | Northwest and west (Sirsa, western Fatehabad, northwestern Hisar) | Dry, sandy tracts with low rainfall |
| Aravalli outcrops | South (Mahendragarh, Rewari, Gurugram, Nuh, Faridabad) | Isolated low ridges, the northern tail of the Aravalli range |

The plain is sometimes split into smaller doab-like tracts between the
Yamuna, the Ghaggar and the Sutlej region. Exam questions mostly ask
only the four-region list and the location of the Bagar.

[example]
A question asks: "In which district would you find the Bagar region?" Link
Bagar with the dry northwest, so Sirsa (and parts of Fatehabad and Hisar)
is correct, not Panchkula (hills) or Faridabad (Aravalli edge).
[/example]

## 3. Rivers, Seasonal Streams and Canals

Haryana has only one perennial river on its boundary, the **Yamuna**. Most
other streams are rain-fed and seasonal.

| River / stream | Notes |
| --- | --- |
| Yamuna | Forms the eastern boundary with Uttar Pradesh; a tributary of the Ganga |
| Ghaggar (Ghaggar-Hakra) | The main seasonal river; enters near Pinjore, flows past Ambala and Sirsa, then disappears into Rajasthan; associated with the old Saraswati channel |
| Markanda | Seasonal; ancient name Aruna; flows into the Sarsuti and then the Ghaggar system |
| Sarsuti | Seasonal stream linked in tradition with the Saraswati |
| Tangri | Seasonal tributary of the Ghaggar system, Ambala region |
| Chautang | Seasonal; identified by many with the ancient Drishadvati |
| Sahibi | Seasonal, rises in the Aravallis; later the Najafgarh drain in Delhi |
| Dohan, Krishnavati, Indori | Seasonal tributaries of the Sahibi in the south, often lost in the sand |
| Somb, Kaushalya, Dangri | Smaller Ghaggar-system streams of the north |

**Canals.** Because rivers are scarce, canals matter. The **Western Yamuna
Canal** is the oldest; its earliest version was dug by Firoz Shah Tughlaq
in the 14th century and was later restored by the British in 1817. It
takes water from the Yamuna at the Tajewala/Hathnikund headworks in
Yamunanagar district and serves the northern and central districts. Other
important sources are the **Bhakra** system and the
**Sutlej-Yamuna Link (SYL)** canal, which is a long-running dispute with
Punjab.

[definition]
**Barrage** means a low diversion structure with gates to control flow
into canals; it is not a large storage dam. The Hathnikund and Tajewala
barrages on the Yamuna and the **Ottu barrage** on the Ghaggar (Sirsa) are
the best-known examples.
[/definition]

## 4. Dams, Lakes and Protected Areas

| Place | District | Type or fame |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Hathnikund / Tajewala | Yamunanagar | Yamuna barrage headworks for the Western Yamuna Canal |
| Pathrala | Yamunanagar | Barrage on the Somb stream |
| Ottu | Sirsa | Barrage on the Ghaggar |
| Kaushalya dam | Panchkula | Small dam on the Kaushalya stream |
| Anangpur dam | Faridabad | Ancient dam near Surajkund |
| Karna Lake | Karnal | Lake and bird-watching spot on the highway |
| Badkhal Lake, Surajkund | Faridabad | Lake and pond-based tourist sites |
| Damdama Lake | Gurugram | Lake in the Aravalli foothills |
| Blue Bird Lake | Hisar | Artificial lake and picnic spot |
| Tilyar Lake | Rohtak | Lake and zoo complex |
| Sultanpur National Park | Gurugram | Bird sanctuary, a wetland for migratory birds |
| Kalesar National Park | Yamunanagar | Sal forest park, notified as a national park in 2003 |
| Bhindawas Wildlife Sanctuary | Jhajjar | Large wetland sanctuary |

## 5. Climate, Soils and Vegetation

The state has a **semi-arid to sub-humid** climate with four seasons:
a hot summer (peaking in May-June, around 40 degrees Celsius or above), a
monsoon from late June to September, a short post-monsoon and a cool
winter (lowest in December-January). Rainfall decreases from northeast to
southwest. The Shivalik belt receives the most, and the Bagar and
Mahendragarh areas the least. The state's average rainfall is only about
590 mm, and the monsoon brings most of it; western disturbances add winter
showers that help rabi crops.

**Soils.** Broadly, the plains have alluvial and loamy soils that support
wheat and rice, while the southwest has light sandy soils that are prone
to wind erosion. Hill tracts have thin, stony soils.

**Vegetation.** Natural vegetation is thorny, dry deciduous forest and
scrub: shisham, khair, babul and neem are common. Forest cover is very low,
only a few per cent of the state's area, far below the national average
and the 33 per cent target of the National Forest Policy. The plains have
been converted to farm land.

## 6. Exam Traps and Memory Tricks

- Highest point is Karoh in the Shivaliks, not an Aravalli hill.
- The Ghaggar is seasonal; only the Yamuna is the true perennial river.
- Sahibi, Dohan, Krishnavati and Indori all belong to the southern, Aravalli-fed group. Remember them as "South four".
- Ottu barrage is on the Ghaggar at Sirsa; Hathnikund is on the Yamuna.
- Kalesar (Yamunanagar) and Sultanpur (Gurugram) are the national parks.

[summary]
Haryana covers about 44,212 sq km (1.4 per cent of India), is landlocked
and borders Punjab, Himachal Pradesh, Uttar Pradesh, Rajasthan, Delhi and
Chandigarh. Its four regions are the Shivalik hills (Karoh Peak is the
highest point), the Yamuna-Ghaggar alluvial plain, the semi-desert Bagar
and the Aravalli outcrops. The Yamuna is the only perennial river, while
the Ghaggar and the others are seasonal. Canals such as the Western Yamuna
Canal and barrages at Hathnikund and Ottu carry water. The climate is
semi-arid with scanty rainfall (about 590 mm) and low forest cover.
[/summary]
